{"causes":["Thermus's family connections and social status gave him access to influential networks and opportunities for advancement."],"effects":["The implementation of the Lex Minucia, a significant reform aimed at reducing debt and promoting economic stability in Rome, is often attributed to Thermus's efforts as consul in 189 BCE."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"rome","sourceName":"Rome","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus served as consul in Rome from 189 to 188 BCE."},{"sourceSlug":"marius","sourceName":"Marius","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus worked closely with Gaius Marius, a prominent Roman general and statesman."},{"sourceSlug":"thermopylae","sourceName":"Thermopylae","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus fought in the Battle of Thermopylae during his military campaigns."},{"sourceSlug":"cassius-holtonius","sourceName":"Cassius Holtonius","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus was a member of the Roman Senate and served on the council with Cassius Holtonius."},{"sourceSlug":"proconsul","sourceName":"Proconsul","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus was appointed as a proconsul in Asia Minor, exercising authority over Roman territories beyond Italy."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-republic","sourceName":"Roman Republic","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus served during the late Roman Republic, a period marked by significant social and political upheaval."},{"sourceSlug":"cicero","sourceName":"Cicero","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus was mentioned in Cicero's writings as a prominent figure in Roman politics."},{"sourceSlug":"rome-empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"Thermus lived during the early years of the Roman Empire, marking a significant turning point in its history."},{"sourceSlug":"minucian-law","sourceName":"Minucian Law","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"quintus-minucius-thermus","targetName":"Quintus Minucius Thermus","context":"The Minucian Law, a major reform aimed at regulating Roman society, was heavily influenced by Thermus's ideas and policies."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome","role":"Capital"},{"name":"Thermopylae","role":"Battlefield"},{"name":"Asia Minor","role":"Proconsular Territory"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Quintus Minucius Thermus","type":"Reference article","slug":"quintus-minucius-thermus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q940749","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quintus_Minucius_Thermus_(consul_193_BC)"],"tags":["Political Leaders","political-systems","geopolitical-linkage","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Quintus Minucius Thermus (fl. 200s BCE; consul 193 BCE; died c. 186 BCE) was a Roman Republican politician and military commander of the early 2nd century BCE — a praetor, consul, and general who participated in the Roman military campaigns of the Ligurian Wars and post-Hannibalic consolidation that extended Roman control over northern Italy. His career falls in the critical period of Roman expansion following the Second Punic War (218–201 BCE), when Rome was simultaneously absorbing the territories won from Carthage and Hannibal's Italian allies, pushing northward against the Ligurians of the Apennines and the Gallic tribes of the Po valley, and projecting power eastward into the Greek world (the Macedonian and Syrian Wars).

Quintus Minucius Thermus served as praetor (probably c. 196 BCE) and was elected consul for the year 193 BCE — one of the two men who, according to Roman constitutional convention, held supreme civil and military authority in the Roman Republic. During his consulship he was assigned the province of Liguria, where the Roman legions were engaged in a prolonged pacification campaign against the Ligurian peoples of the northwestern Apennines — a low-intensity but persistent conflict that occupied numerous consuls throughout the first decades of the 2nd century BCE. His military operations in Liguria secured him a minor but recognised place in the Roman military record.

Although Quintus Minucius Thermus is not among the major figures of Roman Republican history — his career lacks the dramatic scale of contemporaries like Scipio Africanus, Flamininus, or Marcus Porcius Cato — he represents the typical senior magistrate of the period: a member of the Roman political elite who moved through the standard career progression (cursus honorum), held military command in the active frontier zones, and contributed to the incremental expansion and consolidation of Roman power in northern Italy.","subjects":["Roman Republic","Ancient Rome","Classical Era","Roman Military","Italy","2nd Century BCE","Republican Rome","Roman History"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Thermus's leadership and diplomatic efforts played a crucial role in maintaining stability during the late Roman Republic, setting a precedent for future generations of Roman leaders.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
